We consider an optimal-control problem with state constraints and terminal cost, where we aim to minimize energy dissipation over time by controlling two scalar functions on $\mathbb{R}^d$, subject 
   to partial differential equations (PDEs) that model nematic fluid flow under certain assumptions about its physical properties; see \cite[Section 1]{Ericksen}. The PDE constraint can be written as one equation or several coupled ones depending upon whether it models uniaxiality ($1$-dimensional symmetry), biaxality $(0$-$dimensinal symmetry), etc.; here our focus will only include systems modeling 2-$dimensionally symmetric fluids which are also called ``biaxially'' ordered liquids because they have no preferred direction along any axis but rather exhibit planar alignment within their layers when viewed at some fixed point through crossed polarizers (\textit{\`a la} Figure~\ref{fig_intro}). We assume these materials possess isotropic viscosity coefficients so there exists just three independent parameters describing them---namely bulk modulus ($\kappa K$), splay coefficient $(\nu)$, twist constant/coefficient($\tau$) ---which determine how much each component contributes towards overall deformation during motion due either external forces applied externally via boundaries such surface tension effects acting internally between neighboring cells inside tissues like those found throughout living organisms including human beings themselves! This means if you want your material behave differently than what's already been observed then change values accordingly until desired behavior occurs again after making adjustments based off previous observations.\footnote{%
